186050987 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1031 "The Power and Root Rules for Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1033 37 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050987 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7734519e-e666-5361-81f3-89b86b5bc28d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105563 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105563 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $p(x)=9x^4$ and $q(x)=3x^4$, what is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3977d627-65bc-5f36-8c25-7d47b515f383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105563 #edb/ref 1074 "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105564 #edb/ref 1069 "e-36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105564 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(-12x^{12}\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88c456d9-e5d3-5b33-a847-03f201751c19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105564 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph $y=x^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^{12} = \\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-12$ causes $y=-12x^{12}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-12{x}^{12})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105565 #edb/ref 1069 "e-37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105565 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105565 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "551f2aa0-8589-5725-abe6-de1b7eca0327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105565 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=x^3$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty } x^3 = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105576 #edb/ref 1069 "e-39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105576 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow -\\infty} (44-3y^4-9y^{12}) \\, .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105576 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "969d5f07-c5ce-5601-b2de-0d23eb15809b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105576 #edb/ref 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }(44-3{y}^{4}-9{y}^{12}) & ={lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }(-9{y}^{12}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }({y}^{12}) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233871 #edb/r